From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: REG-Linux <cjom@reglinux.org>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] libretro: support loading a second game in the same process

The libretro build assumes the core image is reloaded between games, so a
great deal of its state is initialised exactly once and never again:
retro_deinit() is the only place that tears MAME down, and several file-scope
buffers are only correct while they still hold the zero-filled BSS they start
life as.

A frontend that keeps the core resident in its own process - REG-Station's
in-process player does - calls retro_unload_game() and then retro_load_game()
again with no retro_deinit()/retro_init() in between. Every one of those
assumptions then breaks, in the order you meet them:

  * ARGUC is file scope and only ever incremented, so parse_cmdline() appends
    the second game's tokens to the first game's and MAME is handed the
    command line twice. The second unadorned argument needs <UNADORNED1>,
    which an arcade driver without a software list does not expose:
      Error: unknown option: pacman

  * parse_cmdline() copies each word into ARGUV[] byte by byte and stops at
    the word's length, and parseSystemName()/parseParentPath() strncpy() an
    exact count; none of them terminates. A shorter second value inherits the
    tail of the first:
      Searching for driver: sf2cen        ("sf2ce" over "pacman")
      Parent path = "/var/runa/roms/mame" ("/var/run" over "/userdata/roms/mame")
    parsePath() does terminate, except that its quoted branch writes the NUL
    at slashIndex while the copy ended quoteIndex+1 bytes earlier.

  * mame_machine_manager is a process-wide singleton and cli_frontend::
    execute() skips `delete manager` under __LIBRETRO__, so the second run
    reuses the first run's manager - holding the finished run's emu_options
    and osd_interface - and calls start_luaengine() on it again. That re-runs
    lua_engine::initialize() against a fully populated lua_State; re-
    registering a usertype replaces the sol2 usertype_storage<T> held in the
    registry, and the orphan's __gc runs during that same registration pass:

      ~usertype_storage_base()
      destroy_usertype_storage<lua_engine::bitmap_helper<bitmap_ind32>>()
      GCTM / luaC_step
      lua_pushcclosure()
      sol::stack::set_field<meta_function, closure>()
      lua_engine::initialize()

    which segfaults. When the collector happens not to run there the state is
    left inconsistent instead and the fault lands later, in lua_load() reading
    plugins/boot.lua.

  * retro_unload_game() latches retro_pause to -1 so a retro_run() arriving
    after the teardown cannot step a machine that is gone, and retro_init() is
    the only thing that ever cleared it. The second game therefore builds and
    configures correctly and then never advances: retro_run() skips
    retro_main_loop() every frame and retro_audio_queue() drops every sample.
    POSTNOTIFY (holds MACHINE_NOTIFY_FRAME back so the hiscore plugin can
    finish initialising) and first_run (drops the initial gray-bordered frame)
    are stale in the same way.

  * mmain() keeps its retro_options in a function-local static, and
    core_options can take a value from a command line but has no notion of
    dropping one the next command line leaves out. A game launched with
    "-plugins -plugin hiscore -pluginspath ..." hands those to the next game,
    which passes no plugin options at all, so plugins_path is back to its
    default and start_luaengine() calls fatalerror():
      Fatal error: Could not load plugin: hiscore
    Every option leaks this way; the plugin one is just the one that is fatal.
    The OSD has the mirror problem - a new retro_osd_interface per run, the
    previous one dropped - so the two get the same owner.

  * a run that fails to load leaves all of that behind with nothing to
    collect it. retro_unload_game() is where the teardown belongs, but the
    API does not ask a frontend to unload a game that never loaded, so it is
    never called. The next retro_load_game() builds its command line for a
    manager that already exists and keeps the failed run's emu_options, so
    MAME runs the failed game again rather than the requested one:

      Starting game from cmd: "mslugx -cfg_directory ..."
      l1_mortal_kombat_3_u2_music_spch.u2 NOT FOUND (tried in mk3)

    and start_luaengine() goes back over the same lua_State every launch
    until the collector lands on an orphaned usertype and the segfault above
    happens after all.

Fixes, in the same order:

  * reset ARGUC at the top of parse_cmdline(), the way Set_Default_Option()
    already resets its sibling XARGC
  * terminate every one of those copies
  * do the teardown from retro_unload_game() instead of waiting for
    retro_deinit(), gated on retro_load_ok, which it clears so a frontend that
    reaches retro_deinit() cannot repeat it; null the freed globals and reset
    firstgame so the next load starts from a fresh manager, machine and
    lua_State
  * reset retro_pause, POSTNOTIFY and first_run in retro_load_game(), next to
    retro_load_ok - they are per-run state, and retro_load_game() is the one
    function that runs once per run under every frontend
  * allocate retro_options and retro_osd_interface per run and release them
    from retro_finish(), after the machine and manager that reference them
  * release the same four from retro_load_game() when the load fails, minus
    the machine exit phase - running_machine::run() already ran that from its
    catch block, and repeating it would write the NVRAM of a machine whose
    ROMs never loaded over the good one

Frontends that reload the core image between games are unaffected: they reach
retro_deinit() with retro_load_ok already false and everything is rebuilt from
scratch as before. One thing does change for them - retro_machine_exit() now
runs at unload rather than at deinit, so NVRAM and .cfg are saved at the point
the game actually ends.

Signed-off-by: REG-Linux <cjom@reglinux.org>
---
--- a/src/frontend/mame/clifront.cpp
+++ b/src/frontend/mame/clifront.cpp
@@ -57,6 +57,9 @@
 {
 	util::archive_file::cache_clear();
 	delete retro_manager;
+	// mame_machine_manager::s_manager is cleared by the destructor, so the next
+	// mame_machine_manager::instance() call builds a new one from scratch
+	retro_manager = nullptr;
 }
 #endif
 
--- a/src/frontend/mame/mame.cpp
+++ b/src/frontend/mame/mame.cpp
@@ -37,10 +37,10 @@
 extern int RLOOP;
 extern int ENDEXEC;
 extern mame_machine_manager *retro_manager;
-extern core_options *retro_global_options;
 extern void retro_loop(running_machine *machine);
 extern void retro_execute();
 extern void free_man();
+extern void free_retro_globals();
 
 static running_machine *retro_global_machine;
 static const machine_config *retro_global_config;
@@ -69,18 +69,46 @@
 
 void free_machineconfig()
 {
-	retro_manager->set_machine(nullptr);
+	if (retro_manager)
+		retro_manager->set_machine(nullptr);
 
 	delete retro_global_machine;
+	retro_global_machine = nullptr;
 	delete retro_global_config;
+	retro_global_config = nullptr;
 }
 
-void retro_finish()
+static void retro_release()
 {
-	retro_global_machine->retro_machine_exit();
-
 	free_machineconfig();
 	free_man();
+
+	// machine and manager both reference these, so they go last
+	free_retro_globals();
+
+	// so a fresh manager gets a fresh run of the empty driver
+	firstgame = true;
+	mfirst = false;
+}
+
+void retro_finish()
+{
+	if (retro_global_machine)
+		retro_global_machine->retro_machine_exit();
+
+	retro_release();
+}
+
+/* The run never got off the ground - the manager, the machine, the options and
+   the OSD all exist, but the machine was never handed to the frontend. Drop
+   them without the exit phase: running_machine::run() already ran it from its
+   own catch block, and retro_machine_exit() would save the NVRAM of a machine
+   whose ROMs never loaded over the good one on disc. This is what the non-
+   libretro build does with them on any error - manager, machine and config are
+   all scope-bound there and go away whatever run() returned. */
+void retro_abandon()
+{
+	retro_release();
 }
 
 void retro_main_loop()
--- a/src/osd/libretro/libretro-internal/libretro.cpp
+++ b/src/osd/libretro/libretro-internal/libretro.cpp
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@
 /* forward decls / externs / prototypes */
 
 extern void retro_finish();
+extern void retro_abandon();
 extern void retro_main_loop();
 
 int POSTNOTIFY     = 5; /* See retro_loop() */
@@ -946,6 +947,21 @@
 {
    retro_load_ok = false;
 
+   /* retro_unload_game() latches retro_pause to -1 so that a retro_run() call
+      arriving after the teardown does not step a machine that is already
+      gone. Only retro_init() ever cleared it again, which is fine when the
+      frontend reloads the core between games but leaves the core paused
+      forever when it keeps it resident. Clear it here so every load starts
+      unpaused, whether or not retro_init() ran in between. */
+   retro_pause = 0;
+
+   /* Per-run startup state that retro_init() would otherwise be the only
+      thing to set: POSTNOTIFY holds MACHINE_NOTIFY_FRAME back for the first
+      few frames so the hiscore plugin can finish initialising, and first_run
+      drops the initial gray-bordered frame. */
+   POSTNOTIFY = 5;
+   first_run  = true;
+
    check_variables();
 
 //FIXME: re-add way to handle OGL
@@ -987,6 +1003,20 @@
       log_cb(RETRO_LOG_DEBUG, "%s osd_sleep\n", __func__);
       osd_sleep(osd_ticks_per_second());
       log_cb(RETRO_LOG_DEBUG, "%s osd_sleep done\n", __func__);
+
+      /* A failed load leaves the manager, the machine, the options and the
+         OSD of the attempt behind, and the frontend has no reason to call
+         retro_unload_game() for a game that never loaded - the API does not
+         ask it to - so nothing else will ever free them. The next
+         retro_load_game() then finds mame_machine_manager::instance() already
+         populated and hands it the new command line for nothing: the manager
+         keeps the emu_options of the failed run, so MAME runs that game again
+         (Metal Slug X reported "l1_mortal_kombat_3_u2_music_spch.u2 NOT FOUND
+         (tried in mk3)"), and start_luaengine() re-runs
+         lua_engine::initialize() over the same lua_State until sol2's
+         usertype garbage collector segfaults. */
+      retro_abandon();
+
       return false;
    }
 
@@ -1006,6 +1036,23 @@
 
    if (retro_pause == 0)
       retro_pause = -1;
+
+   /* Tear MAME down here instead of waiting for retro_deinit(): the core is
+      not necessarily unloaded between games. A frontend that keeps the core
+      resident in its own process calls retro_load_game() again with no
+      retro_deinit() in between, and mame_machine_manager is a process-wide
+      singleton, so the next run would reuse the previous run's manager. That
+      re-runs lua_engine::initialize() on a lua_State that is already
+      populated, which orphans the sol2 usertype storage of the first run and
+      segfaults in its garbage-collector finalizer.
+
+      retro_deinit() still calls retro_finish() for frontends that get there
+      first; the retro_load_ok guard keeps it to one teardown. */
+   if (retro_load_ok)
+   {
+      retro_finish();
+      retro_load_ok = false;
+   }
 }
 
 size_t retro_serialize_size(void)
--- a/src/osd/libretro/libretro-internal/retro_init.cpp
+++ b/src/osd/libretro/libretro-internal/retro_init.cpp
@@ -198,10 +198,15 @@
       return 0;
 
    if (quoteIndex > -1)
+   {
       strncpy(gamePath, path + (quoteIndex + 1), slashIndex - (quoteIndex + 1));
+      gamePath[slashIndex - (quoteIndex + 1)] = 0;
+   }
    else
+   {
       strncpy(gamePath, path, slashIndex);
-   gamePath[slashIndex] = 0;
+      gamePath[slashIndex] = 0;
+   }
 
    strncpy(gameName, path + (slashIndex + 1), dotIndex - (slashIndex + 1));
    gameName[dotIndex - (slashIndex + 1)] = 0;
@@ -236,6 +241,7 @@
       return 0;
 
    strncpy(systemName, path + (slashIndex[1] + 1), slashIndex[0] - slashIndex[1] - 1);
+   systemName[slashIndex[0] - slashIndex[1] - 1] = '\0';
    return 1;
 }
 
@@ -276,9 +282,15 @@
       return 0;
 
    if (quoteIndex > -1)
+   {
       strncpy(parentPath, path + (quoteIndex + 1), slashIndex[1] - (quoteIndex + 1));
+      parentPath[slashIndex[1] - (quoteIndex + 1)] = '\0';
+   }
    else
+   {
       strncpy(parentPath, path, slashIndex[1]);
+      parentPath[slashIndex[1]] = '\0';
+   }
    return 1;
 }
 
@@ -708,6 +720,16 @@
    char *p2 = NULL;
    char *start_of_word = NULL;
 
+   /* ARGUC is file scope and is only ever incremented below. A frontend that
+    * keeps the core loaded across games calls retro_load_game() again without
+    * a fresh retro_init(), so without this reset the new tokens are appended
+    * to the previous game's and MAME receives the command line twice. */
+   ARGUC = 0;
+
+   /* For the same reason the two token copies below terminate what they write:
+    * they only ever store the word's own bytes, so they used to depend on
+    * ARGUV still being the zero-filled BSS it is for the first game only. */
+
    strcpy(buffer, argv);
    strcat(buffer, " \0");
 
@@ -742,6 +764,7 @@
                //... do something with the word ...
                for (c2=0, p2 = start_of_word; p2 < p; p2++, c2++)
                   ARGUV[ARGUC][c2] = (unsigned char)*p2;
+               ARGUV[ARGUC][c2] = '\0';
                ARGUC++;
 
                state = DULL; /* back to "not in word, not in string" state */
@@ -756,6 +779,7 @@
                /*... do something with the word ... */
                for (c2=0,p2 = start_of_word; p2 <p; p2++,c2++)
                   ARGUV[ARGUC][c2] = (unsigned char) *p2;
+               ARGUV[ARGUC][c2] = '\0';
                ARGUC++;
 
                state = DULL; /* back to "not in word, not in string" state */
--- a/src/osd/libretro/retromain.cpp
+++ b/src/osd/libretro/retromain.cpp
@@ -210,6 +210,30 @@
 //============================================================
 
 retro_osd_interface *retro_global_osd;
+static retro_options *retro_global_options;
+
+/* Release the options and OSD of the run that just ended. Called from
+   retro_finish(), after the machine and the machine manager are gone - both
+   hold references into these two objects, so they have to outlive them.
+
+   They used to be a function-local static and a per-call leak respectively,
+   which is invisible under a frontend that reloads the core image between
+   games but wrong under one that keeps it resident: emu_options only knows how
+   to take a value from a command line, never how to drop one that the next
+   command line leaves out. So a game launched with "-plugins -plugin hiscore"
+   handed those on to the next game, which was then killed by
+
+     Fatal error: Could not load plugin: hiscore
+
+   because it had no reason to pass -pluginspath anywhere near the plugin.
+   Every option is affected; the plugin one is just the one that is fatal. */
+void free_retro_globals()
+{
+	delete retro_global_osd;
+	retro_global_osd = nullptr;
+	delete retro_global_options;
+	retro_global_options = nullptr;
+}
 
 // translated to utf8_main
 int mmain(int argc, char *argv[])
@@ -241,15 +265,21 @@
 #endif
 
 	{
-		static retro_options retro_global_options;
-		retro_global_osd = new retro_osd_interface(retro_global_options);
+		/* Start every run from a virgin options object. Both it and the OSD
+		   outlive this function - the running machine holds references to
+		   them and mmain() returns while the machine is still running - so
+		   they are owned by free_retro_globals() above, not by this scope.
+		   Do not free here: at this point the previous run's manager, if one
+		   somehow survived, would still be referencing them. */
+		retro_global_options = new retro_options;
+		retro_global_osd = new retro_osd_interface(*retro_global_options);
 
 		retro_output retrooutput;
 		osd_output::push(&retrooutput);
 
 		retro_global_osd->register_options();
 
-		res = emulator_info::start_frontend(retro_global_options, *retro_global_osd, args);
+		res = emulator_info::start_frontend(*retro_global_options, *retro_global_osd, args);
 
 		osd_output::pop(&retrooutput);
 
